# GNU tar 1.12, compiled by the tcc this chain built, against ntlibc.
#
# The PE32 counterpart of ../../../linux/bootstrap/gnutar/build.sh, and a
# different shape: that one runs tar's own ./configure under bash, this one
# asserts the answers and names the compiles.  See the head of default.nix for
# why -- in short, tar 1.12's configure asks about tape drives and remote
# shells, and would get three of its answers wrong here.
#
# ${NIX_BUILD_TOP}, not ${TMPDIR}: wine drops TMPDIR on the way into the
# Windows environment, so it arrives empty and cd is left with no argument.

cd ${NIX_BUILD_TOP}

${bin_ungz} --file ${tarball} --output tar.tar
${bin_untar} --file tar.tar

cd ${NIX_BUILD_TOP}/tar-${version}

# Three patches, none of them about the C library and none of them from
# live-bootstrap, which has no tar recipe.  Two of the three are the same
# observation from opposite ends -- NT does not have the filesystem tar was
# written for -- and the third is cosmetic but earns its place in a bootstrap
# where a diagnostic is the only debugging channel there is.
#
#   nt-no-symlinks       ntlibc's symlink() works, and that is the problem.
#                        Under Wine it produces something Wine reads back as
#                        a symbolic link and nothing else does -- a
#                        zero-length file with '?' on the end of its name --
#                        and on real NT it needs a privilege the build may
#                        not hold.  Withdrawing S_ISLNK puts tar into a
#                        configuration it already supports, and decides that
#                        once rather than per host.
#   nt-readonly-attribute
#                        a file created without owner write picks up NT's
#                        read-only attribute, after which its timestamps and
#                        attributes can no longer be set -- so extracting any
#                        archive containing a 0444 file, which is nearly every
#                        source tarball, failed on both the utime and the
#                        chmod.  Create writable, chmod last.  As of the
#                        ntlibc pinned here chmod() and utimensat() clear the
#                        attribute themselves and both calls succeed on a
#                        0444 file, so this patch no longer carries the build;
#                        it stays because creating writable and applying the
#                        real mode last is right whatever the C library does,
#                        and because it is what makes the order independent
#                        of that library's version.
#   nt-program-name      argv[0] arrives as a full NT path.
#
# Each file says the rest in its own header.
${patch} -Np0 -i ${patchSymlinks}
${patch} -Np0 -i ${patchReadonly}
${patch} -Np0 -i ${patchProgramName}

# What ./configure would have written -- see the head of config.h.  It lands
# in the tree root, which is where -I. finds it.
${bin_cp} ${configH} config.h

# Everything is compiled from the tree root rather than from src/ and lib/ in
# turn, so that one -I list serves both: this tcc does not search an included
# file's own directory, so src/*.c need -I src for "system.h" and "common.h",
# and lib/*.c need -I lib for "error.h" and "backupfile.h".  The twenty-six
# basenames are distinct across the two directories, so the objects can all
# land here without colliding.
#
# The order of the -I list is load-bearing, and this is the one place this
# package differs from grep in how it is spelled.  tar's own lib comes BEFORE
# ntlibc's include directory, because two of the headers there have to win:
#
#   fnmatch.h  ntlibc's is POSIX and stops at FNM_PATHNAME, FNM_NOESCAPE and
#              FNM_PERIOD.  names.c and buffer.c ask for FNM_LEADING_DIR, the
#              GNU flag that makes a pattern match everything beneath a
#              directory it names; without it `tar -xf a.tar dir' extracts
#              the directory and nothing in it, and --exclude excludes one
#              entry rather than a subtree.  lib/fnmatch.c is compiled below
#              and implements the flag.  Because it is a plain object and not
#              an archive member, it satisfies the reference before libc.a is
#              consulted at all and ntlibc's fnmatch.o is never pulled in --
#              so there is no duplicate definition, only a choice.
#   getopt.h   tar's declares the same struct option as ntlibc's, in the same
#              order, with the same three has_arg constants; but tar.c reads
#              it for nothing else, and taking tar's keeps the include list
#              from depending on which header happens to come first.
#
# HAVE_CONFIG_H is the only -D: system.h and the lib/*.c files include
# <config.h> only when told to.  Everything else configure would have put on
# the command line is in config.h instead, because make and kaem here run a
# command line without a shell and a -D whose value contains a quote cannot
# be written on one.
alias CC="${tcc} -c -I. -Ilib -Isrc -nostdinc -I${ntlibcSrc}/include -I${ntlibcSrc}/arch/i386 -I${ntlibcSrc}/arch/generic -I${ntlibc}/include -B ${ntlibc}/lib -DHAVE_CONFIG_H"

# The fifteen files of tar proper, as src/Makefile.am names them.
CC -o arith.o src/arith.c
CC -o buffer.o src/buffer.c
CC -o compare.o src/compare.c
CC -o create.o src/create.c
CC -o delete.o src/delete.c
CC -o extract.o src/extract.c
CC -o incremen.o src/incremen.c
CC -o list.o src/list.c
CC -o mangle.o src/mangle.c
CC -o misc.o src/misc.c
CC -o names.o src/names.c
CC -o open3.o src/open3.c
CC -o rtapelib.o src/rtapelib.c
CC -o tar.o src/tar.c
CC -o update.o src/update.c

# And eleven of libtar.  lib/Makefile.am names twelve; getopt.c and getopt1.c
# are gone because ntlibc has getopt, getopt_long and getopt_long_only, and
# fnmatch.c is here although lib/Makefile.am lists it only as EXTRA_DIST --
# configure adds it when the system fnmatch does not work, and by tar's
# standard ntlibc's does not.  See the -I note above.
#
# getdate.c is compiled from the pre-generated parser the tarball ships.
# That is worth naming because the Linux coreutils on this chain had to give
# up its copy: coreutils 5.0's getdate.c is a modern bison output that
# nothing here can compile, and touch lost its -d parser as a result.  tar
# 1.12's is fifteen years older and compiles clean, so `tar --newer DATE'
# keeps its argument parser.  What it parses it in is the C locale and, with
# no timezone database under this library, in UTC.
#
# The eight files NOT compiled from lib/ are the AC_REPLACE_FUNCS set --
# alloca.c, basename.c, dirname.c, execlp.c, fileblocks.c, ftruncate.c,
# gmalloc.c, memset.c, mkdir.c, rename.c, rmdir.c, stpcpy.c and strstr.c.
# ntlibc has every one of those functions, and this is the point where the
# decision is made; config.h does not bother to say HAVE_STPCPY because
# nothing would read it.  This is also the safe way round: had any of these
# been compiled and put in an archive ahead of libc.a, tar's version would
# have won silently rather than colliding.
CC -o argmatch.o lib/argmatch.c
CC -o backupfile.o lib/backupfile.c
CC -o error.o lib/error.c
CC -o fnmatch.o lib/fnmatch.c
CC -o getdate.o lib/getdate.c
CC -o getversion.o lib/getversion.c
CC -o modechange.o lib/modechange.c
CC -o msleep.o lib/msleep.c
CC -o xgetcwd.o lib/xgetcwd.c
CC -o xmalloc.o lib/xmalloc.c
CC -o xstrdup.o lib/xstrdup.c

# One link, and no libtar.a at all: the Linux build archives lib/*.o and puts
# libtar.a on the link line, which on this side would be an archive ahead of
# libc.a and so a place for tar's copy of something to win by accident.
# Twenty-six plain objects have no such ordering to get wrong.
#
# A PE-target tcc adds neither a startup object nor a default C library, and
# the default it would reach for (msvcrt, kernel32) is not what this bootstrap
# has, so crt1.o and -lc/-lntdll are named outright -- ntdll.def being the
# import library ntlibc resolves its kernel calls through.  -lc -lntdll come
# last, after tar's own objects, which is what a linker that resolves archives
# in one pass needs.  The Linux build's -static has no meaning here.
#
# src/rmt.c is not built.  It is the remote-tape server, and configure only
# builds it where <sys/mtio.h> and <sys/socket.h> both exist; there is neither
# here, and no rsh for rtapelib to reach it with either.
${tcc} -B ${ntlibc}/lib -nostdlib ${ntlibc}/lib/crt1.o -o tar.exe arith.o buffer.o compare.o create.o delete.o extract.o incremen.o list.o mangle.o misc.o names.o open3.o rtapelib.o tar.o update.o argmatch.o backupfile.o error.o fnmatch.o getdate.o getversion.o modechange.o msleep.o xgetcwd.o xmalloc.o xstrdup.o -L ${ntlibc}/lib -lc -lntdll

# Run it before installing it, on tar's real job rather than on --version.
#
# The archive is tar's own source: forty-odd files over two directories, a
# range of sizes, and -- the case that made the read-only patch necessary --
# a handful of files the tarball ships mode 0444.
#
# The check at the end is `tar -d', which is the strongest one available with
# no diff, no cmp and no shell in the picture: it re-reads the archive and
# compares every member against the extracted copy, byte for byte, and
# reports size, contents, mode and modification-time differences separately.
# kaem is --strict, so any of those fails the build.  Between them the four
# commands cover create, list, extract into a fresh tree, and verify.
./tar.exe -cf self.tar lib src
./tar.exe -tf self.tar
${bin_mkdir} -p verify
cd verify
../tar.exe -xf ../self.tar
../tar.exe -df ../self.tar
cd ..

# It lands as tar.exe, as bash, make, patch, sed and grep do -- ntlibc's PATH
# search tries the bare name before name.exe, so a script asking for `tar'
# still finds it.
${bin_mkdir} -p ${out}/bin
${bin_cp} tar.exe ${out}/bin/tar.exe
